{
  "id": "ep053",
  "duration": 47.434,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.281,
      "text": "Twenty-five million space rocks slam into Earth's atmosphere every single day.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "GMT235 16 44 Jeanette Epps 1068 ReboostBlur (iss071e547091).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Johnson Space Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GMT235_16_44_Jeanette_Epps_1068_ReboostBlur_(iss071e547091).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.067,
          "end": 5.267,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.281,
      "end": 10.301,
      "text": "We call these bright flashes shooting stars, but they are not distant stars at all.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Perseids (20358214388).jpg",
        "credit": "Paul VanDerWerf from Brunswick, Maine, USA",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perseids_(20358214388).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.267,
          "end": 7.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 7.133,
          "end": 10.3,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.301,
      "end": 15.371,
      "text": "While drifting through deep space, a tiny rock fragment is known as a meteoroid.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "243 ida crop.jpg",
        "credit": "243_ida.jpg: NASA/JPL\nderivative work:  \u00a0Chzz\u00a0\u00a0\u25ba",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:243_ida_crop.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.3,
          "end": 15.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 15.371,
      "end": 20.566,
      "text": "The moment it plunges into our atmosphere and ignites, it becomes a fiery meteor.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Comet Lemmon Oct 30.png",
        "credit": "Chuck Ayoub",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 15.367,
          "end": 17.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 17.967,
          "end": 20.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.566,
      "end": 26.986,
      "text": "Major showers like August's Perseids happen when Earth plows directly through dusty comet trails.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "2018 Perseid No 2 (44004016011).jpg",
        "credit": "Mike Lewinski",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Perseid_No_2_(44004016011).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.567,
          "end": 23.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 23.8,
          "end": 27.0,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.986,
      "end": 33.906,
      "text": "Most meteoroids vaporize completely, but any surviving piece that hits the surface becomes a meteorite.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Esquel.jpg",
        "credit": "Doug Bowman",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esquel.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 27.0,
          "end": 30.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 30.467,
          "end": 33.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 33.906,
      "end": 41.881,
      "text": "Surprisingly, some of these retrieved meteorites are actual pieces of planetary debris blasted off Mars or the Moon.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NWA 6963 full slice.jpg",
        "credit": "Steve Jurvetson",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NWA_6963_full_slice.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 33.9,
          "end": 41.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 41.881,
      "end": 47.084,
      "text": "That constant rain of ancient space debris is why night skies stay filled with motion.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "GMT235 16 44 Jeanette Epps 1068 ReboostBlur (iss071e547091).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Johnson Space Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GMT235_16_44_Jeanette_Epps_1068_ReboostBlur_(iss071e547091).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 41.867,
          "end": 47.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
